{
217: {skill_tested: "Identify the narrator's initial error in Passage A.", question: "At first, the narrator of Passage A treats the pruning notebook primarily as:", option1: "a record too incomplete to consult.", option2: "a collection of Elena's observations.", option3: "a fixed schedule to repeat.", option4: "proof that Tree 18 was dying.", correctAnswer: "a fixed schedule to repeat.", explanation: "Step 1: The notebook lists earlier dates and actions.<br><br>Step 2: The narrator repeats a prior year's cut on the corresponding date without checking present buds.<br><br>Answer: a fixed schedule to repeat.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Character and Relationship Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
218: {skill_tested: "Identify a stated function of an unusual tool.", question: "According to Passage B, Aunt Jo used the worn wooden sticks to:", option1: "measure the length of replacement wires.", option2: "apply colored wax to guaranteed repairs.", option3: "hold dial cords while glue dried.", option4: "listen for vibrations without touching a live component.", correctAnswer: "listen for vibrations without touching a live component.", explanation: "Step 1: Mr. Bell identifies one of the sticks.<br><br>Step 2: He explains that Aunt Jo placed it against a cabinet and near her ear to trace vibration safely.<br><br>Answer: listen for vibrations without touching a live component.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
219: {skill_tested: "Identify a shared theme across paired memoirs.", question: "Which idea is central to both passages?", option1: "Inherited materials become useful through interpretation that includes context and other people's knowledge.", option2: "Written labels preserve every skill needed to continue a practice.", option3: "An earlier generation's methods should be copied without alteration.", option4: "Uncertain evidence is best discarded immediately.", correctAnswer: "Inherited materials become useful through interpretation that includes context and other people's knowledge.", explanation: "Step 1: Passage A combines the notebook with buds, weather, and Elena's instruction.<br><br>Step 2: Passage B combines tools with demonstrations and community memories.<br><br>Answer: Inherited materials become useful through interpretation that includes context and other people's knowledge.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
220: {skill_tested: "Analyze the significance of missing records.", question: "In Passage A, the crowded crowns during the grandfather's illness primarily demonstrate that:", option1: "the grandfather intentionally wanted every crown crowded.", option2: "blank pages can correspond to meaningful interruptions in practice.", option3: "weather notes are less useful than dated commands.", option4: "trees stop growing when no one writes about them.", correctAnswer: "blank pages can correspond to meaningful interruptions in practice.", explanation: "Step 1: The notebook lacks entries during the grandfather's illness.<br><br>Step 2: Several trees show the physical effects of missed pruning.<br><br>Step 3: Absence in the record is connected to an absence of action and is therefore informative.<br><br>Answer: blank pages can correspond to meaningful interruptions in practice.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
221: {skill_tested: "Analyze a narrator's change in classification.", question: "Why does the narrator of Passage B stop sorting objects primarily by appearance?", option1: "All objects that look obsolete are secretly valuable antiques.", option2: "Mr. Bell asks that every duplicate remain in the shop.", option3: "An unfamiliar-looking object may have a specialized use recognizable only through practice.", option4: "Photographs make physical objects unnecessary.", correctAnswer: "An unfamiliar-looking object may have a specialized use recognizable only through practice.", explanation: "Step 1: The wooden stick appears disposable to the narrator.<br><br>Step 2: Mr. Bell demonstrates its specialized diagnostic use.<br><br>Step 3: Visual unfamiliarity is therefore an unreliable substitute for investigating practice.<br><br>Answer: An unfamiliar-looking object may have a specialized use recognizable only through practice.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Character and Relationship Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
222: {skill_tested: "Compare the role of supporting characters.", question: "Elena in Passage A and the visitors in Passage B serve similar roles by:", option1: "proving that the narrators should surrender all decisions.", option2: "providing complete written instructions for both practices.", option3: "arguing that inherited objects should never be changed or discarded.", option4: "showing the narrators how to interpret inherited records beyond their literal surfaces.", correctAnswer: "showing the narrators how to interpret inherited records beyond their literal surfaces.", explanation: "Step 1: Elena connects dates to current buds and weather.<br><br>Step 2: Visitors connect tools and markings to remembered use.<br><br>Step 3: Both turn static-looking artifacts into evidence embedded in practice.<br><br>Answer: showing the narrators how to interpret inherited records beyond their literal surfaces.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
223: {skill_tested: "Analyze how narrators preserve uncertainty.", question: "The weather notes in Passage A and qualified labels in Passage B both reflect an effort to:", option1: "add present observations without pretending they form final instructions.", option2: "erase every decision made by the earlier generation.", option3: "replace physical evidence with personal opinion.", option4: "make future interpretation unnecessary.", correctAnswer: "add present observations without pretending they form final instructions.", explanation: "Step 1: The orchard notes record current conditions alongside prior actions.<br><br>Step 2: Radio-shop labels retain terms such as probably and name unknown.<br><br>Step 3: Both narrators contribute evidence while leaving later readers room to reassess it.<br><br>Answer: add present observations without pretending they form final instructions.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
224: {skill_tested: "Synthesize two distinctions between evidence and mastery.", question: "Taken together, the passages suggest that receiving evidence of a practice differs from receiving the practice itself because:", option1: "evidence is useful only when its original owner provides a complete explanation.", option2: "artifacts can support judgment, but skill still develops through observation, comparison, demonstration, and use.", option3: "practical knowledge cannot survive in any material form.", option4: "mastery comes automatically from preserving every old object.", correctAnswer: "artifacts can support judgment, but skill still develops through observation, comparison, demonstration, and use.", explanation: "Step 1: The notebook records past judgments but does not decide current pruning.<br><br>Step 2: Tools preserve traces of repair but do not make the narrator a technician.<br><br>Step 3: In both cases, evidence becomes operational only through situated learning and other people.<br><br>Answer: artifacts can support judgment, but skill still develops through observation, comparison, demonstration, and use.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
225: {skill_tested: "Compare qualified continuity in paired conclusions.", question: "Which choice best describes how both narrators continue the inherited work?", option1: "Each preserves an unchanged system whose meaning is already complete.", option2: "Each abandons the earlier record after discovering it contains gaps.", option3: "Each adds a new interpretive layer while acknowledging dependence on earlier and communal knowledge.", option4: "Each becomes fully expert before altering any label or entry.", correctAnswer: "Each adds a new interpretive layer while acknowledging dependence on earlier and communal knowledge.", explanation: "Step 1: The orchard narrator adds weather notes and sketches in response to the grandfather's judgments.<br><br>Step 2: The shop narrator builds a documented archive from objects and multiple witnesses.<br><br>Step 3: Neither claims complete mastery; continuity consists of responsible extension rather than exact repetition.<br><br>Answer: Each adds a new interpretive layer while acknowledging dependence on earlier and communal knowledge.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Comparative Passage Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"}
}
